1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,140
A watch can survive a dive and still fail its diver.

2
00:00:03,940 --> 00:00:06,000
If you cannot read it, what good is it?

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:08,860
Underwater, the task sounds simple.

4
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:10,120
Look at your wrist.

5
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:11,500
Find the minute hand.

6
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:13,540
Work out how long you have been down.

7
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:17,820
But light changes, your surroundings move, and a dial

8
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:20,340
designed for an office can become a puzzle.

9
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:24,460
The Rolex Submariner was built around that world: a watch

10
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:27,700
case that resisted water, a display that could be read

11
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:31,100
quickly, and a rotating ring that made elapsed time

12
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,340
visible. Those details eventually became a familiar

13
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:35,880
look far beyond diving.

14
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:39,880
How did a practical instrument acquire that kind of reach?

15
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,020
The answer begins with people learning to breathe

16
00:00:43,020 --> 00:00:46,220
underwater. And later, it takes a surprising

17
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:49,580
detour to the deepest part of the ocean, where the

18
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,840
Rolex attached to a submarine was a very different watch.

19
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:58,320
In nineteen forty-three, French engineer Émile Gagnan and

20
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:01,440
naval officer Jacques-Yves Cousteau built an autonomous

21
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:04,860
compressed-air diving apparatus. The device became

22
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:06,080
known as the Aqua-Lung.

23
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:09,920
A diver could carry a supply of air and move without a

24
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:11,720
breathing hose running back to the surface.

25
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,940
That freedom opened possibilities for exploring, filming

26
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:17,620
and working below the waves.

27
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:21,660
During the following decade, scuba diving attracted a

28
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:24,580
wider public. More people were preparing equipment,

29
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,580
boarding boats and discovering a landscape they had

30
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:28,840
previously seen from above.

31
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:31,800
Taking a watch along made practical sense.

32
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:34,760
It supplied a reference for how much time had passed,

33
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:37,480
something a written plan could not show on its own.

34
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:40,080
But a watch was one part of the equipment.

35
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:43,560
Its hands could not report how much air remained in a

36
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:47,020
cylinder. For the watch itself to be useful, its mechanism

37
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:49,760
had to survive the place where its owner wanted to use it.

38
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:54,080
Every ten metres of descent adds roughly another

39
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:57,620
atmosphere of pressure. The farther down an object

40
00:01:57,620 --> 00:02:00,700
goes, the more strongly the surrounding water presses

41
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:04,240
against it. For a wristwatch, that turns small

42
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:06,520
openings into serious design problems.

43
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:11,140
A crown has to move so the owner can set the hands, yet

44
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:13,860
the case around it must keep water away from the

45
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:16,660
mechanism. Rolex already had a foundation.

46
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:20,720
Its Oyster case dated to nineteen twenty-six, using a

47
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:22,660
sealed construction to protect the movement.

48
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:26,800
Developing a diving watch meant taking that idea into a

49
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:29,880
more demanding environment. According to Rolex,

50
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:33,940
Royal Navy divers tested Oyster Perpetual watches during

51
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:37,460
exercises from HMS Reclaim in Scotland's Loch Fyne

52
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:40,780
in nineteen fifty-two. The brand records

53
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:42,620
depths of up to seventy-three metres.

54
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:45,840
That account describes watches working underwater.

55
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:49,400
The next question was how to build their use into the

56
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:49,620
design.

57
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:54,020
Rolex dates the launch of the Submariner to nineteen

58
00:02:54,020 --> 00:02:57,340
fifty-three. Its initial water-resistance rating

59
00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:00,900
was one hundred metres, and its screw-down crown

60
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:03,220
used the Twinlock sealing system.

61
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:07,120
The new watch brought its underwater purpose onto the

62
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:10,800
wrist: a clear dial, prominent hands and a rotating

63
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:12,140
scale around the face.

64
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:15,200
You could look at it and see what it was meant to do.

65
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:18,040
There were other designers pursuing that purpose.

66
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:21,500
Blancpain dates its Fifty Fathoms to the same year.

67
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:24,600
Several companies were shaping the equipment that would

68
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:25,780
become familiar to divers.

69
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:29,820
A claim about being first can overshadow the more useful

70
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:33,260
question: did the design work well for the person using

71
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:36,500
it? A depth rating answered something about the case.

72
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:39,200
It did not answer everything about the display.

73
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:43,360
For that, Rolex's own account points to an engineer who

74
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:44,760
spent time below the surface.

75
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:50,160
Dimitri Rebikoff was an engineer, diver and

76
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:53,780
underwater photographer. According to Rolex, his

77
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:57,000
feedback followed one hundred and thirty-two dives at

78
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,560
depths between twelve and sixty metres.

79
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:02,980
One recommendation concerned a tiny triangle.

80
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:06,120
The marker on the rotating bezel was red.

81
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:10,080
Rebikoff suggested making it white to improve legibility.

82
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:13,760
There is a reason colour behaves differently down there.

83
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:17,640
Water filters light as depth increases, and red

84
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:19,820
wavelengths disappear more quickly than blue.

85
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:22,900
The appearance of an object depends on the light available

86
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:24,160
to reflect from it.

87
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:27,840
The important detail in Rolex's account is the feedback

88
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:31,400
itself. Someone used the watch in the environment it

89
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:34,840
was designed for, then suggested a change to what the

90
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:38,420
diver actually saw. A small marker could carry a

91
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:41,760
large responsibility: letting the wearer find the starting

92
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:44,900
point quickly. Once that starting point was clear,

93
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:47,280
the rotating scale could do its work.

94
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:51,580
Picture a simplified modern dive-watch dial.

95
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:54,640
The minute hand points to twenty past the hour.

96
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:59,020
Rotate the bezel until its zero marker lines up with that

97
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:01,300
hand. Now let the hand move.

98
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:03,560
The bezel stays where you set it.

99
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:07,220
Its scale shows how many minutes have passed since that

100
00:05:07,220 --> 00:05:10,880
starting point. That is the basic idea of the Submariner's

101
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:14,280
elapsed-time bezel. A movable reference sits

102
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:17,580
beside a moving hand, so the wearer can read the interval

103
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:21,200
directly. On today's Submariner, the bezel turns

104
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:24,120
only counterclockwise. If it shifts accidentally,

105
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:28,360
the indication moves toward more elapsed time, rather than

106
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:31,600
less. The design makes that particular error conservative.

107
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:33,520
This is a time display.

108
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:37,620
It does not measure air supply, choose a safe depth or

109
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:39,260
calculate a complete dive plan.

110
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:42,440
But it makes one piece of information easier to read.

111
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:45,600
The next challenge was keeping that information available

112
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:47,340
as the watch itself evolved.

113
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:52,380
Rolex reports that the Submariner's rating reached two

114
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:54,540
hundred metres within a year of launch.

115
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:57,660
Later changes arrived along different paths.

116
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:01,060
The Submariner Date appeared in nineteen sixty-nine.

117
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:04,840
Its rating increased to three hundred metres in nineteen

118
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:08,500
seventy-nine. The model without a date reached that

119
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:10,000
rating in nineteen eighty-nine.

120
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:13,520
Those dates matter because a familiar name can hide

121
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:15,000
several generations of design.

122
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:18,720
A feature on today's watch cannot simply be assigned to

123
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:22,040
the original. The numbers also describe the watch's water

124
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:24,920
resistance. They are not instructions for how deep its

125
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:28,100
owner should go. A useful way to read the progression

126
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:31,560
is as a history of the object: its case, seals

127
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:35,120
and display developed while its basic purpose remained

128
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:38,280
easy to recognise. Yet Rolex's most

129
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:41,700
dramatic ocean story took the brand far beyond those

130
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:45,120
ratings. And it can lead people to mix up two very

131
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:45,760
different objects.

132
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:51,400
On January twenty-third, nineteen sixty, Jacques Piccard

133
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:54,580
and U.S. Navy Lieutenant Don Walsh descended

134
00:06:54,580 --> 00:06:58,200
aboard the bathyscaphe Trieste to Challenger Deep

135
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:01,860
in the Mariana Trench, nearly eleven kilometres below

136
00:07:01,860 --> 00:07:05,500
the surface. A Rolex watch travelled on the outside

137
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:09,180
of the vessel. Rolex identifies it as the Deep

138
00:07:09,180 --> 00:07:12,680
Sea Special, an experimental model designed for

139
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:16,020
enormous pressure. That distinction is essential

140
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:17,660
to the Submariner's story.

141
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:21,280
The watch mounted outside the Trieste was

142
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:22,460
the Deep Sea Special.

143
00:07:23,380 --> 00:07:26,040
The dramatic expedition does not establish an

144
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:29,240
eleven-kilometre rating for a Submariner on someone's

145
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:32,340
wrist. The two objects show different ambitions

146
00:07:32,340 --> 00:07:36,000
within the same company's ocean work: an experimental

147
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,680
instrument facing extreme depth, and a wristwatch

148
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:41,360
designed for use by divers.

149
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:44,700
The title's real question comes back here.

150
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:47,400
How did the Submariner conquer the deep?

151
00:07:48,580 --> 00:07:51,520
Its answer lies in making a useful wrist instrument,

152
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:54,880
while specialised challenges continued to produce

153
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:56,220
specialised watches.

154
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:01,000
The Sea-Dweller, introduced in nineteen sixty-seven,

155
00:08:01,460 --> 00:08:03,660
addressed another requirement of professional diving.

156
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:08,020
In saturation diving, divers spend extended periods in

157
00:08:08,020 --> 00:08:11,540
pressurised environments using breathing mixtures that can

158
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:14,740
include helium. Helium can enter a watch case.

159
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:17,820
When the surrounding pressure falls during decompression,

160
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:21,140
gas trapped inside can create a damaging pressure

161
00:08:21,140 --> 00:08:24,360
difference. Rolex's helium escape valve

162
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:26,600
let that excess gas leave the case.

163
00:08:26,600 --> 00:08:30,260
It was a response to the conditions of a particular kind

164
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:33,860
of work. The broader lesson is that diving equipment

165
00:08:33,860 --> 00:08:35,380
developed through specific problems.

166
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:38,580
One situation demanded a readable time scale.

167
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:42,400
Another demanded a way to release internal gas pressure.

168
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:45,920
Those solutions should not be folded into a single

169
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:47,740
legendary watch with every capability.

170
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:51,340
The Submariner already had a clear purpose of its own.

171
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:54,380
Then its appearance began reaching people who might never

172
00:08:54,380 --> 00:08:56,240
have a reason to descend below the surface.

173
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:01,640
In nineteen sixty-two, Sean Connery wore a Submariner in

174
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:02,760
the first James Bond film.

175
00:09:03,660 --> 00:09:06,600
The watch's recognisable design could now reach a cinema

176
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:09,980
audience as part of a character associated with danger

177
00:09:09,980 --> 00:09:13,500
and confidence. That connection helps explain its cultural

178
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:17,080
life. A practical object can become a symbol when

179
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:19,820
people recognise the activities and characters attached to

180
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:22,320
it. But return to the diver from the opening.

181
00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:25,620
The useful question is still whether the wearer can find

182
00:09:25,620 --> 00:09:28,580
the hand, see the marker and read the interval.

183
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:32,880
The Submariner's story connects those small acts to a much

184
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:36,180
wider world: the growth of scuba, feedback

185
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:39,840
from underwater use, changing case designs and the

186
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:43,360
reach of cinema. Its conquest of the deep is a

187
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:46,680
story of adaptation. A familiar shape carried the evidence

188
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:48,960
of the job it had been designed to do.

189
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:51,560
Water-resistant enough to keep ticking.

190
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:53,340
Clear enough to be read.

191
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:56,800
Recognisable long after the diver returned to the surface.
